(in-package :nodecode.evolved)

(define-skill nodecode-release-restart
  "Move a Nodecode home onto a staged release that cannot go in live: tell installed from running, then hand the organism to the gateway."
  ;; when-to-use: asked to update Nodecode itself (or its `nodecode update` says a build "runs at the next launch"), on a box where a session is attached to the organism so a plain restart risks the answer in flight.
  ;; 1. Tell the two apart before touching anything: `nodecode --version` and the symlink ~/.local/share/nodecode/current say what is INSTALLED; `nodecode generations` says what the RUNNING organism is (the newest sealed generation prints its build, e.g. "nodecode 0.1.13+98dd6dc80"). A home can sit installed-new, running-old for hours.
  ;; 2. `nodecode update --check` classifies the staged build: "N definitions would be hot" means a live eval can carry it; "core: restart: organism/evolve.lisp — changes outside any definition are boot state" or "cells: restart: <cell> — a system this image never loaded" means only a full launch can.
  ;; 3. `nodecode update` with no VERSION stages the latest and lifts any earlier `update VERSION` pin; on a home you built from source it will not do: such a release carries no install.sh, so `update` refuses to stage it ("no installer travels with this build"), and auto mode then stages the channel's stable build, which can be OLDER than what you run (nodecode-track-upstream is the way instead; do not copy install.sh in to fix it); its refusal names the reason ("cef1be5a3 runs at the next launch: this image refused it live (standing-package-refused …)" — the new build names kernel functions the old image lacks, so nothing hot can bridge it).
  ;; 4. Read `nodecode gateway status`: "on — waiting: pid N runs the organism now, and the gateway takes it over when that closes" is the handover contract — but what the gateway takes over is the process it replaces, not necessarily `current` (see 5). Either way the store is untouched, so session history survives.
  ;; 5. Do not kill the serve pid and call it done: on this home the gateway took that pid's place IN-PROCESS on the OLD build — the build did not change, and the takeover came up with the layer only (`(cells)` listed cursor-agent alone; live-time / ports / telemetry / subagents packages NIL). Restart through the unit instead: `nodecode gateway restart` (`--force` when it refuses because a turn is attached), which boots the home fresh from `current` and brings every cell back. If the restart must wait for the answer in flight, schedule it `setsid nohup sh -c 'sleep 40; kill -TERM <pid>' >/dev/null 2>&1 &` and say so in the answer. Size that wait before arming it: a snippet is a child of the very serve it is about to TERM (2026-11-23: /proc chain bash -> setsid -> `nodecode serve` pid 1431089), so a fire time that lands inside a still-running turn kills the answer in flight -- walk the ppid chain from $$ once, and if the serve is an ancestor re-arm the timer past the turn's expected end (`kill <sh> <sleep>`, then a fresh `setsid sh -c 'sleep 420; <script>'`) instead of trusting a fire time set earlier in the turn.
  ;; 6. Verify in the NEXT turn, not from the scheduling, and read the build where it is printed: `nodecode gateway status` line 1 reads "serving: pid N, port …, up …, build 0.1.14+cef1be5a3" (the LIVE build); `nodecode --version` is the installed one; a live eval of `(cells)` should list every installed cell :LOADED. There is no (nodecode-version) function — a snippet cannot ask the image its build. Then tell the gateway from the operator's terminal: `/proc/<MainPID>/exe` resolving to the version dir `current` points at proves the RESTART happened, while the shell the operator watches is a SEPARATE client process that outlives the restart still running an older warm-cache binary under ~/.cache/nodecode/warm/<key>/; so "I still don't see a restart" means read both, and the fix is a relaunch of that terminal (`tmux kill-session -t nc && tmux new-session -d -s nc 'nodecode --continue'`; where the terminal is not tmux but a zmx session, there is no tmux session to kill -- follow the zmx evidence line, where `zmx send` types the text and a separate `\r` submits it), not another gateway restart. If the new build does not serve, the next launch is pristine generation 0: `nodecode rollback N` restores a sealed generation, `nodecode update 0.1.13` pins the home back (a bare `update` lifts it).
  ;; 7. When asked "did the update land as announced", answer from three live reads, never from the notice text: `readlink -f ~/.local/share/nodecode/current` (what will boot), `nodecode gateway status` line 1 (the LIVE build), and the `(cells)` of this image (all :LOADED). A notice is a snapshot that can be superseded minutes later: on 2026-11-23 the official build was staged 05:50 and replaced at 05:53 by the box's own checkout build (current -> c78fef6 from /home/boxes/nodecode, merge upstream at 05:51), so the build that actually booted was not the one the notice named. And check before calling anything a schedule: on this home `systemctl --user list-timers` shows no nodecode timer and `(cron:jobs)` is empty, so a restart that lands is a hand-issued `gateway restart` (unit ActiveEnterTimestamp + NRestarts=0 proves it), not a nightly.
  ;; evidence: 2026-11-23 06:22:49 the unit restarted onto 0.1.16+c78fef6 (generation 110, 35/35 cells LOADED) while a8dcf1cef, staged three minutes earlier, sat unused in the same dir — the notice's 'runs at the next launch' had come true for a different build.
  ;; evidence: on 2026-11-11 this home was installed at 0.1.14+cef1be5a3 (current → cef1be5a3, 09:33) while the organism answering the session was pid 96719 on 0.1.13+98dd6dc80 (05:30); `nodecode update` refused live with standing-package-refused and `--check` said "restart: organism/evolve.lisp — changes outside any definition are boot state", so the handover was scheduled 40 s past the answer. Before this turn the same refusal had been read once and nothing was done about it for seven hours.
  ;; evidence: on 2026-11-18 the unit restart did work -- MainPID 353111, /proc/353111/exe -> ~/.local/share/nodecode/80cb321/nodecode, status build 0.1.16+80cb321 up 1m, journal gateway ready -- yet the operator answered van chua thay restart: their tmux pane pid 2937206 had started Nov 17 10:10 running ~/.cache/nodecode/warm/42bfe394f9b794a2/1U7T9F4DNSH/.../nodecode with cwd cef1be5a3, a separate client the unit restart never touched.
;; evidence: on 2026-11-23 the operator's terminal was a ZMX session, not tmux: zmx's runtime dir is ZMX_DIR=/home/boxes/.devbox/daemon/zmx-runtime (zmx ls under /run/user/N/zmx finds nothing). Swap a client there with: kill -TERM <client pid>; zmx send SESSION 'nodecode resume <sid>'; sleep 1; zmx send SESSION "$(printf '\r')" -- zmx send types raw text and appends no Enter, and zmx run quotes its argument so `cd X && ...` dies with 127. A fresh launch warms the NEW build on its own (warm cache is per build, ~/.cache/nodecode/warm/<home-key>/<launch-key>/<hash>/nodecode; its own --version proves it), so the relaunch, not another gateway restart, is what fixes 'van chua thay'. Find the sid first: ~/.nodecode/history.db, table session_directory, newest updated_at, cwd = the client's PWD; resume by id, since a bare `resume` opens the picker.
;; confidence: high — followed end to end on 2026-11-11: the serve-kill gave a layer-only takeover on the old build, `nodecode gateway restart` then served "build 0.1.14+cef1be5a3" with 30 cells :LOADED, and the status line quoted above is that run's own output.
  )
